welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is the right honorable raelia omalo  Odinga Prime Minister of the Republic of  Kenya from 2008 to 2013 he was first  elected as the Member of Parliament for  Langara in 1992 served as Minister of  Energy from 2001 to 2002 and as Minister  of roads Public Works and housing from  2003 to 2005 mr. Dingle ran against  incumbent Moy Kibaki into the 2007  presidential election while the results  of that election were largely in  question mr. Kibaki was announced the  winner and mr. Odinga accused mr. Kibaki  of electoral fraud two months of  violence in the country ensued before  Kofi Annan the United States Secretary  General at the time intervened and  brokered a deal that provided for power  sharing between mr. Odinga and mr.  Kibaki and the recreation of the post of  Prime Minister today we'll talk with mr.  Odinga about the state of democracy in  Africa welcome your excellency I think  you think you realize oh I'm happy to be  here good good  there are 54 sovereign countries in  Africa and most of all of them are  members of the United Nations and all  except for Morocco are members of the  African Union how many would you  consider to be full democracies well  it's not what I consider but what the  world generally considers I think they  say that about 22 of these 54 countries  on the continent  certain degree democratic governments to  a certain degree yeah yeah because the  condition in these countries is not a  uniform right it varies from country to  country but as you know that Africa is  in transition right so these are very to  classified as the leaders in the process  of democratization right right and where  does can you fall in that group I think  I can't fault the ending that group just  said lower lower end of it okay yeah so  for the remaining 54 countries what do  you think what are the major obstacles  to democracy taking root in those  countries in Africa well you know there  are areas where there have been  reversions  because you still have the old order  clinging to power that letting go as you  know what do you manage to do following  the the fall of Berlin Wall and the wind  of change that blew in Africa probably  bringing down them single-party and  military dictatorships the those are the  countries where you still have the old  leadership which just refused to let go  participating in elections perfunctorily  but refusing to allow the free and fair  elections in other words they reinvent  themselves they perpetuate themselves  some of them have also refused to accept  the timely limitations when the time  limitation comes they manipulate the  Constitution allow themselves to  continue to  power and is that primarily for economic  reasons I think is more for economic and  personal reasons and they were also  refused to liberalize for example the  society the media freedom is not there  so some of the repressive laws of the  past are still in place in those  countries  okay the youth population in Africa more  than 50% of the population in Africa are  nineteen years or younger in fact it's  Africa is the the has the most  population with the highest rate of  youth do you think that that huge group  can can have any sway in terms of  democracy yes what can they do you know  the youth can be a blessing or a curse  depending on on how you look at it the  young population which is empowered can  be a force for growth and development on  the other hand if the youth is not  empowered then they remain impediments  to economic development because they  will grow into delinquents drug addicts  criminals and so on so we are saying  that this young population in Africa  needs to be empowered so that you can be  able to leave the demographic dividend  like China has done like India's also  done if they are empowered they can  become a serious force for change also  like you saw in the Arab Spring yes  in Tunisia in Egypt and so on if they're  livin attended to they become a major  major force for political  destabilization yes how do you empower  the youth you empower the youth through  skills development that we impart skill  to the youth first you need to make  education accessible and affordable  right from the childhood necessary  primary and high school education and  then also have institutions that will  impart skill to these youths so that  they can become a skilled laborers this  is what China did so that china became  the factory of the world attracting  investors to come into the country to  get industries which requires cheap and  skilled labor force this is my view is  what we need to do in a vector so of the  54 countries and of course only 22 are  our true democracies you know how do you  get education to the youth of those  countries that are not democracies I  mean it seems very difficult yeah first  you know that there are the Millennium  Development Goals we supposed to be  attained by the 2015 I think when the  record was taken it appears as most of  these countries are not going to achieve  the MDGs particularly as far as  education is concerned but I think it is  an issue which a you the African Union I  need to really push we had as far as  education is concerned and so that you  have a common standard one  I believe we need to make the education  as I mentioned accessible from the  Carroll Allen from the nursery school to  primary and then to secondary so that  every child irrespective of his or her  parent social status has access to  quality education like any other child  from primary then you also address the  issue of the transition from at each  level from primary to secondary because  usually have in some country very high  dropout levels at primary and then also  very high dropout at the secondary level  those who don't transition to tertiary  training or universities and because of  that they become basically useless to  society we must address the training at  all those levels that those who don't  make it to the universities have  opportunity to go to tertiary training  institutions they kind of get skills  they can become nurses they can become  technicians and they can become  electricians plumbers as also for that  let's you mentioned the Africa the  African Union and helping with education  how strong is the African Union the  African Union could be stronger okay I  think that the issues we need to address  which they are not addressing such as  what like I just mentioned achievement  of MDGs I think that a you needed to set  up a mechanism through which they can  monitor the performance of its members  in those regards  okay you have a new part of course  which is part of the AU but I don't  think that sufficient emphasis is being  put there that the nations develop  common standards you see  EU is doing that before you become a  memory you your vetted there are certain  specific conditions that you must meet  right before you admitted to the  membership yeah in the AU just a matter  of course so people don't understand why  they should enjoy those privileges I  think that being a member of a you  should have certain obligations  people don't just sit comfortable that  you run a risk of losing your membership  for example if you don't run your  economy in this way or the other way  because in my view we should address the  issue or moving towards economic  integration of Africa like similar to  the EU yes yes and how do you think  other countries would feel about that in  Africa I know that other countries may  not be very comfortable with this but  but I am sure that we should not remain  too complacent as au Rock has been the  case because he was supposed to be an  improvement on the OE u co au was more  or less like a liberation movement which  was formed to continue the process of  liberating Africa from coronary yoke and  appetite well that was achieved then it  was disbanded and formed into we owe you  au  the only you of course did not mind  about what is happening within the in  internally among these member states  there's a clause which was called  non-interference in the internal affairs  of member states so a member state could  violate Human Rights Idi Amin could kill  so many people in Uganda  Mobutu in Congo banned in Malawi  perhaps our intent of the Republic au  was opposed to an improvement because  instead of non-interference there is a  clause which says non indifference to  violation of human rights in each member  state right but that is more in print  than in practice I was going to say no  one seems to be interfering with any of  the violence that are going in on in  some parts of Africa today so I mean  there are a number of issues in Africa I  mean what do you think is the very first  thing that needs to happen to move  toward democracy in those parts in those  countries that are not democratic well  without being specific I think that the  a you need to apply the stick by looking  at the performance of some of those  countries and insisting that certain  conditions must be met  you see we tend to wait until too late  when the war has broken out but when you  find a running left right and center  trying to broker peace but my view is  that you  be proactive so that you can avoid  breakdown of law and order in a country  we just recently you have seen conflict  escalate in Mali in the Central African  Republic in South Sudan each time the EU  gets involved when it is too late  because they are waited until their  secret shall become explosive but one  knows the conditions which eventually  lead to those kind of conflicts so they  need to be addressed much earlier before  the the reach those levels which end up  causing too much suffering to children  to remain until the elderly when the  country is in a civil war so do you have  hope for the future of Africa and  democracy happening someday in your  lifetime in those countries where it  does not happen now yes